  <Document>
    <name>🌡️ LIVE AIR TEMPERATURE</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 6, 2026 9:31PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE AIR TEMPERATURE</h2><p><b>Gradient key</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/air_temperature/legend.png" width="600" alt="gradient key"><br>2 m air temperature (degF), FIXED key (-60..150 F) with a STEADY gradient on the map: the raster blends smoothly through blue-white extreme cold &rarr; gray-blue subfreezing &rarr; <b>royal-blue freezing wall (30-40)</b> &rarr; frigid turquoise/blues (40-55) &rarr; transitional lime/teals (55-70) &rarr; mellow yellows (70-85) &rarr; warming orange/golds (85-100) &rarr; <b>scorching pink/reds (100+) to HIGHEST+ 150</b>, passing exactly through every key color (key bars show the <b>LOWEST -60</b> to <b>HIGHEST+ 150</b> band centers). Same temperature always shows the same color; every color is brightened so nothing renders dark. Freezing (32 F) sits inside the royal-blue wall. Record <b>LOWEST 26.02</b> / <b>HIGHEST+ 89.09</b>.</p><p>This entry auto-refreshes from the live overlay (source: NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km 2 m temperature analysis, hourly cycles).</p><p>Turn on/off independently of all other layers. Add this file once; new source cycles appear automatically.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
